==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Cubism}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|cubisme}}. One story is that, in 1908, as a new canvas by [[w:George Braque|Braque]] was being carried past, someone said, “''Encore des Cubes! assez de cubisme!''”. The quotations below ascribe the coinage to [[w:Henri Matisse|Matisse]].

See also the word {{m|fr|cube|}} (from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|cubus}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|κύβος}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈkjuːb.izəm/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|often|capitalized}} An [[artistic]] [[movement]] in the early 20th Century characterized by the depiction of [[natural]] [[form]]s as [[geometric]] structures of [[plane]]s.
#* '''2003''', ''The New Yorker'', 3 March,
#*: Matisse coined the name '''Cubism''' as a derisive joke.
#* '''2005''', ''The New Yorker'', 29 Aug, p. 78,
#*: A few recall that, in 1908, he [Matisse] inspired the coinage of the term '''“cubism,”''' in disparagement of a movement that would eclipse his leading influence on the Parisian avant-garde.

